{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G4WUjReRNQftTPew94RSFk","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nMakes `ard serve` check GitHub connectivity and permissions at startup, accept a personal access token for live runs, and fall back to the `gh` CLI token when no credentials are set. It also updates the proposal, README and runbook. The work touches auth, serve startup, config check, possibly the reviewer and merger, and docs. Only the engineer triage role exists, so it plans all of it.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: implementation steps and test plan for the startup check, PAT live mode, `gh` fallback with redaction, the self-review behaviour and the doc updates.\n- worker engineer: Go code changes across internal/github, internal/servecmd, internal/configcmd and possibly internal/reviewer and internal/merger, with tests against the GitHub fake and a fake `gh`, plus doc edits.\n\n## Open issues\n- The issue leaves one choice to triage: how self-review works when ard runs on a PAT. Either ard posts a COMMENT review carrying the verdict and the merger gates on ard's own record, or the docs require a second identity for reviews. The engineer plan should pick one. A maintainer may want to confirm it, because it changes the merge gate.\n- The bot-commit audit workflow under .github/ should only be documented, not changed. Agents may not modify .github/, and it is a protected path.\n- There are no architect, product-manager or documentation-planner roles, but this change is user-visible and crosses several packages. The engineer plan has to cover those concerns too.\n5:Decision on self-review (the one open choice): no reviewer change is needed. `internal/reviewer` already posts every review with event COMMENT (`github.ReviewInput` is \"always posted with event COMMENT\"), and ard's verdicts are gated on its own `.ard/<issue#>/` records, not on GitHub approvals. The only approval gate is the merger's protected-path check, `approved()` in `internal/merger/gates.go`, which already ignores reviews by `self` (ard's login). With a PAT, `self` is the token's user, so that user's own approval is ignored (GitHub also forbids approving your own PR). Behaviour: **a PAT setup needs protected-path approvals from a different write-access user**; the docs say so and a test pins it. A maintainer may want to confirm this.\ndocs/proposal.md:503:**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` is written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.\ndocs/proposal.md:505:**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\ndocs/proposal.md:602:- [x] **`ard init` and `ard setup` (#303).** Decided 2026-11-24: `init` owns the content of `.ard/` (local tree by default, `--pr` for the `ard-init` PR, upgrades tracked in the user-editable `.ard/ard-init.lock`), and `setup` applies the local `.ard/` to GitHub, writing only `ard:*` labels and `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` and only checking rulesets, the bypass list, merge settings, App permissions and the bot-commits workflow. See the two paragraphs under Configuration. Implemented by #344 to #349; #350 adds `--create-gh-app`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19b3a639-14a4-4624-a1cf-f8eaa6796990","uuid":"5438500a-70aa-4a21-8c66-47b71014f468","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:00:45.167Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":